From 1f484b46c4a2083b4e217dbdd03e5a5a27b63caa Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: brunduk <nikolai.viktorovich@gmail.com>
Date: Mon, 17 Aug 2026 18:45:50 -0300
Subject: [PATCH] d/wmr: revert the accel half of the left-hand reflection --
 measured precession (negative result)

A figure-8 test was decisive: with gyro+accel both negated the left
controller winds up whole extra turns instead of closing (a flipped gravity
reference fighting a correct gyro never settles -- precession), while gyro-only
had all three axes rotating correctly with just a constant heading offset left.
The reconciliation algebra's 'coherent diag(1,-1,1) on both sensors' inference is
hereby refuted by hardware; gyro-only stands. The heading offset is the separate
no-absolute-yaw-reference problem.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
 src/xrt/drivers/wmr/wmr_controller_hp.c | 11 +++++++++++
 1 file changed, 11 insertions(+)

diff --git a/src/xrt/drivers/wmr/wmr_controller_hp.c b/src/xrt/drivers/wmr/wmr_controller_hp.c
index 3f95c3b46..0aca8c0e7 100644
--- a/src/xrt/drivers/wmr/wmr_controller_hp.c
+++ b/src/xrt/drivers/wmr/wmr_controller_hp.c
@@ -405,6 +405,17 @@ wmr_controller_hp_packet_parse(struct wmr_controller_hp *ctrl, const unsigned ch
 	if (wcb->base.device_type == XRT_DEVICE_TYPE_LEFT_HAND_CONTROLLER &&
 	    debug_get_bool_option_wmr_controller_left_yaw_gyro_invert()) {
 		last_input->imu.gyro.y = -last_input->imu.gyro.y;
+		// MEASURED NEGATIVE RESULT, do not re-add (2026-08-17): negating the
+		// accel Y here too -- which the frame-reconciliation algebra suggested as
+		// the "coherent" diag(1,-1,1) on both sensors -- made the left controller
+		// PRECESS: a 3D figure-8 wound up whole extra turns instead of closing
+		// (a wearer reported it kept making full extra turns), while the right's
+		// closed exactly.
+		// A flipped gravity reference fighting a correct gyro never settles. The
+		// empirical truth is gyro-only: with just the gyro negation a wearer
+		// reported all three axes rotating correctly, leaving only a constant
+		// heading offset -- which is the separate
+		// no-absolute-yaw-reference problem, not a frame error.
 	}
 
 	uint32_t prev_ticks = last_input->imu.timestamp_ticks & UINT32_C(0xFFFFFFFF);
